  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "xEprJ3EVQVDmSktBADzD7NvpNC0=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:28:45.993Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "Magenta's products, OS2mo and OS2borgerPC, serve Danish municipalities through the OS2 cooperative. The data involved is public-sector organisational data \u2014 which units exist, who belongs to them, with what role. The research notes that auditors must be able to reconstruct it, which is a claim about history rather than about present correctness.\n\nThe gap follows from that. Organisational structures change continuously, and a system holding the present structure cannot answer what the structure was on a given date or who changed it. That is precisely the question asked when a decision taken under an earlier arrangement is later challenged, and it cannot be answered by a more accurate snapshot.\n\nThe commercially decisive fact here is the channel rather than the gap. Because these products are distributed through the cooperative, one integration reaches many municipalities instead of one client. It is the same federated-buyer shape as the Dutch cooperatives elsewhere in the corpus, and it changes how much engineering effort a single integration can justify.\n\n---\n\n## Leads sharing this finding (1)\n\n**2 \u00b7 next**\n- Magenta ApS (DK Copenhagen + GL) \u2014 Morten Kj\u00e6rsgaard, CEO \u00b7 info@magenta.dk \u00b7 https://magenta.dk\n\nVerbatim research text this claim was extracted from:\n\n> OS2mo and OS2borgerPC serve Danish municipalities through the OS2 cooperative \u2014 public-sector organisational data that auditors must be able to reconstruct. The OS2 route means one integration reaches many municipalities.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-19T11:28:45.993895Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:28:45.993899Z"
    }
  },
